At midnight, a silver hare stepped from the frost, starlight caught in its whiskers. “What is guarded grows lonely,” it said. “What is given learns to become a road.”

Luma climbed the cedar past owls, cones, and trembling nests, until stars shivered above her like scattered grain. Darkness rose, immense and gentle. “Light will reveal cracked roofs, empty bowls, and hidden tears.”

Luma opened her wings. “Then it will reveal the hands ready to mend, fill, and comfort.”

The ember flew east and burst into golden feathers. Dawn poured over rivers, roofs, faces, and fields. Darkness curled beneath leaves, becoming shade and dreams.

That is why beetles gleam at sunrise: they honor Luma’s first gift.

