Myth: Before sunrise learned to speak, the world slept beneath a blue-black veil, and rivers found the sea by trusting stones. In a hollow of windless chimneys lived Sera, a keeper of lamp oil. At her belt swung a pearl shell, and within it glimmered the last ember, red as a fox’s eye, warm as bread.

Villagers came carrying cold lanterns. Children hid their fingers in sleeves. Reeds bowed beneath frost, and seeds dreamed of spring. Sera clasped the shell shut. “If I give this little fire away, the night will make a home inside me.”

At midnight, a silver hare crossed the snow, its whiskers bright with stars. “A hidden flame grows smaller,” it said. “A shared flame remembers how to multiply.”

Sera climbed the black hill until the sky trembled like a field of seeds. Darkness rose before her, deep and gentle. “Light will uncover broken roofs, empty bowls, and sorrow folded behind doors.”

Sera opened the shell. “Then it will uncover hammers, ovens, and arms ready to hold.”

The ember leapt east and broke into golden wings. Morning flowed over rivers, faces, roofs, and furrows. Darkness settled under trees as shade, dreams, and rest.

Since then, every dawn candle honors Sera, who gave one spark and received the world back.
